/* data/learn-c/mth-11-05.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/mth-11-05.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-11-05"] = {"9.1":{"s":"Probability starts with a random experiment, whose result cannot be predicted but whose possible results are known. The set of all possible results is the sample space. Any part of it is an event. A single result is a simple event. Two events are mutually exclusive if they cannot happen together, and exhaustive if one of them must happen. The complement of an event A is the event that A does not happen.","w":["Mutually exclusive means no common result. Independent is a different idea and does not mean the same."],"x":["A random experiment is an experiment whose result cannot be told in advance, but whose possible results are all known: tossing a coin, throwing a die, drawing a card. One performance of it is a trial.","The set of all possible results is the sample space S. Throwing a die gives S = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}. Any subset of S is an event. An event with a single result is a simple event. The event happens if the result of the trial is one of its members.","Two events are mutually exclusive if they cannot happen together (they have no common result), like getting an odd number and an even number in one throw. Events are exhaustive if at least one of them must happen. The complement A′ of an event A is the event that A does not happen, so A and A′ are mutually exclusive and exhaustive. Events are equally likely if none is favoured over another.","Common mistake: confusing mutually exclusive with independent. Mutually exclusive events cannot happen together, which makes them strongly dependent. Independent events do not affect each other."],"e":"Throw a die. S has 6 results. The event A = {2, 4, 6} is 'an even number'. Its complement A′ = {1, 3, 5}. The events {1, 2} and {5, 6} are mutually exclusive."},"9.2":{"s":"The probability of an event is a number between 0 and 1 that measures how likely the event is. An impossible event has probability 0 and a sure event has probability 1. For equally likely results, it is the number of results favourable to the event divided by the total number of results in the sample space. The probability of the whole sample space is 1.","f":[{"x":"0 <= P(A) <= 1","n":"Probability of any event A lies between 0 and 1.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.212"},{"x":"P(S) = 1","n":"The probability of the whole sample space S is 1.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.212"}],"w":["A probability can never be negative or more than 1; if you get such an answer, recheck the count."],"x":["The probability of an event measures how likely it is to happen. If all results of a trial are equally likely, then P(A) = (number of results favourable to A)/(total number of results in S).","Properties: 0 ≤ P(A) ≤ 1 for every event. An impossible event has probability 0, and a sure event has probability 1. The probability of the whole sample space is 1: P(S) = 1. The probability of the complement is P(A′) = 1 − P(A), which is often the quickest way to find 'at least one' probabilities.","How to count: for dice, cards and coins list the sample space or use counting (permutations and combinations). For two dice, the sample space has 36 equally likely pairs. For a pack of 52 cards, there are 4 suits of 13 cards, with 12 face cards.","A probability can never be negative or greater than 1. If you get such a value, recheck the count. Common mistake: counting the same outcome twice, or treating results that are not equally likely as if they were."],"e":"Two dice are thrown. Probability that the sum is 7: the favourable pairs are (1,6), (2,5), (3,4), (4,3), (5,2), (6,1), so 6 of 36. P = 6/36 = 1/6."},"9.3":{"s":"The addition theorem gives the probability that at least one of two events happens. Add the two probabilities, then subtract the probability that both happen, because that overlap was counted twice. If the events are mutually exclusive, the overlap is zero and the probabilities simply add. In words: A or B means the union of the two events.","f":[{"x":"P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A ∩ B)","n":"At least one of A, B happens. For mutually exclusive events P(A ∩ B) = 0.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.212"}],"w":["Do not forget to subtract P(A ∩ B) unless the events are mutually exclusive."],"x":["The addition theorem gives the probability that at least one of two events A and B happens (A or B, the union): P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B). The overlap, where both happen, is counted in both P(A) and P(B), so it is subtracted once.","If A and B are mutually exclusive, then P(A ∩ B) = 0, and the probabilities simply add: P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B). For three events or more that are mutually exclusive, add all of them.","Often it is quicker to use the complement: P(at least one) = 1 − P(none). For example, the probability of getting at least one head in three tosses is 1 − (1/2)³ = 7/8.","Common mistake: forgetting to subtract P(A ∩ B) for events that can happen together, such as drawing a heart and drawing a king from a pack. The king of hearts is in both."],"e":"One card is drawn from 52. P(heart or king) = 13/52 + 4/52 − 1/52 = 16/52 = 4/13. The king of hearts is counted once."},"9.4":{"s":"Conditional probability is the probability of an event when we already know that another event has happened. Knowing that B has occurred shrinks the sample space to B. So the probability of A given B is the probability that both happen divided by the probability of B. It is defined only when P(B) is not zero. Reversing the roles of A and B gives the other form.","f":[{"x":"P(A/B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B)","n":"Probability of A given that B has occurred; P(B) is not zero.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.212"}],"w":["P(A/B) and P(B/A) are different numbers. Check which event is the condition: it goes in the denominator."],"x":["Conditional probability is the probability of an event A when we already know that event B has happened. It is written P(A/B). Knowing B has happened reduces the sample space to B, so P(A/B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B), which needs P(B) not equal to zero.","In the same way, P(B/A) = P(A ∩ B)/P(A). The two are different numbers: the event after the slash is the condition, and it goes in the denominator.","For equally likely results, P(A/B) can also be found by counting inside B: the number of results in A ∩ B divided by the number of results in B. This is often the easiest way.","Common mistake: swapping A and B. For two dice, the probability that the sum is 8 given that the first die shows 4 is 1/6 (only the pair (4,4) works among six pairs with first die 4). The probability that the first die is 4 given that the sum is 8 is 1/5, which is different."],"e":"Two coins are tossed. Given that at least one head appears, find P(both heads). S = {HH, HT, TH, TT}. B = {HH, HT, TH} has 3 results, and A ∩ B = {HH} has 1. So P(A/B) = 1/3."},"9.5":{"s":"The multiplication theorem gives the probability that two events both happen: the probability of the first times the probability of the second given the first. If the events are independent, the occurrence of one does not change the chance of the other, so the probabilities simply multiply. If A and B are independent, then A and the complement of B are also independent.","f":[{"x":"P(A ∩ B) = P(A)*P(B/A)","n":"Both events A and B happen.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.212"},{"x":"P(A ∩ B) = P(A)*P(B)","n":"Only for independent events A and B.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.212"}],"w":["Use the simple product P(A)P(B) only when the events are independent, such as separate coin tosses.","Drawing without replacement makes the events dependent: the second probability changes."],"x":["The multiplication theorem gives the probability that two events both happen: P(A ∩ B) = P(A) × P(B/A). First A happens, and then B happens, knowing that A has happened.","Events A and B are independent if the happening of one does not change the probability of the other, that is, P(B/A) = P(B). Then P(A ∩ B) = P(A) × P(B). Separate tosses of a coin, or throws of a die, are independent. If A and B are independent, then A and B′, A′ and B, and A′ and B′ are also independent.","For dependent events, such as drawing cards or balls without replacement, the second probability changes after the first draw. Use the product with the conditional probability: P(two red balls from a bag) = P(first red) × P(second red given first red).","Common mistake: using P(A) × P(B) when the events are dependent. Ask: does the first result change the numbers for the second? If yes, use the conditional probability."],"e":"A bag has 4 red and 6 blue balls. Two balls are drawn one after the other without replacement. P(both red) = (4/10) × (3/9) = 12/90 = 2/15."},"9.6":{"s":"Bayes' theorem reverses a conditional probability. Suppose the events E1, E2, up to En are mutually exclusive and together cover the whole sample space. If an event A has happened, the probability that it came through a particular Ei is found by dividing the probability of reaching A through that Ei by the total probability of A over all the Ei.","f":[{"x":"P(E_i/A) = P(E_i)*P(A/E_i)/(P(E_1)*P(A/E_1) + ... + P(E_n)*P(A/E_n))","n":"E_1, ... E_n are mutually exclusive and exhaustive, with P(A) greater than 0.","r":"Balbharati Maths XI Part 1, p.212"}],"w":["The denominator is the total probability of A: add the product for every Ei, not just the one asked for.","Draw a tree diagram first; the events E must be mutually exclusive and exhaustive."],"x":["Bayes' theorem finds the probability of a cause when we know the effect. Suppose E₁, E₂, …, Eₙ are mutually exclusive and exhaustive events (they cover the whole sample space, with no overlap), and A is an event that can happen only together with one of them. Then P(Eᵢ/A) = P(Eᵢ) P(A/Eᵢ) divided by [P(E₁)P(A/E₁) + … + P(Eₙ)P(A/Eₙ)].","The denominator is the total probability of A, which is the sum over all causes of P(Eⱼ) × P(A/Eⱼ). The numerator is the part that comes through the cause we are asking about.","Method: (1) name the causes E₁, E₂, … and the observed event A; (2) write P(Eᵢ), the prior probabilities, and P(A/Eᵢ), the chance of A under each cause; (3) draw a tree diagram, with a branch for each cause and then a branch for A; (4) find the product on each branch that ends in A; (5) divide the branch you want by the sum of all such branches.","Common mistakes: using only one term in the denominator, and mixing P(A/Eᵢ) with P(Eᵢ/A)."],"e":"Machine 1 makes 60 percent of the items, with 2 percent defective. Machine 2 makes 40 percent, with 5 percent defective. A defective item is found. P(it is from machine 1) = (0.6 × 0.02)/(0.6 × 0.02 + 0.4 × 0.05) = 0.012/0.032 = 3/8."},"9.7":{"s":"Odds compare the chance of an event happening to the chance of it not happening, written as a ratio. Odds in favour of A are the ratio of the favourable results to the unfavourable results. Odds against A are the same ratio turned round. Odds can be converted to probability and back, which is how problems on odds are solved.","f":[{"x":"Odds in favour of A are m : n ⇒ P(A) = m/(m + n)","n":"Odds in favour"}],"w":["Odds 3:2 in favour mean 3 favourable out of 5 total outcomes, not a probability of 3/2."],"x":["Odds compare the chance that an event happens with the chance that it does not. If an event A has m favourable results and n unfavourable results (all equally likely), the odds in favour of A are m : n, and the odds against A are n : m.","Converting: if the odds in favour of A are m : n, then P(A) = m/(m + n), and P(A′) = n/(m + n). If P(A) = p, then the odds in favour are p : (1 − p). For example, odds of 3 : 2 in favour mean a probability of 3/5, with 5 total equally likely results.","Method for problems: write the odds as a ratio of two whole numbers, add them to get the total, and then find the probability. The odds against are the ratio turned round.","Common mistake: reading the odds 3 : 2 as a probability of 3/2, which is impossible. The probability is 3/5."],"e":"The odds against an event are 5 : 3. So the odds in favour are 3 : 5, and P(event) = 3/(3 + 5) = 3/8. The probability that it does not happen is 5/8."}};
